Easily. The only thing you need to know is C and some information how 
item information is stored in NetHack.

The relevant code is addinv() function in invent.c (lines 300-340, the 
relevant lines are 318-322):

317:   /* merge if possible; find end of chain in the process */
318:   for (prev = 0, otmp = invent; otmp; prev = otmp, otmp = otmp->nobj)

Start loop with otmp pointing to the first item in the hero's inventory 
and advance otmp to the next object in inventory until otmp doesn't 
point to anything.

319:      if (merged(&otmp, &obj)) {
320:         obj = otmp;
321:         goto added;

Try to merge the item to be added (pointed by obj) to the item in 
inventory (pointed by otmp) if succesful exit the loop by jumping to the 
label added:

322:      }

End of the for-loop

The second part of adding to heros inventory is the function merged() in 
invent.c (lines 156-230, the only relevant line being 163):

163:	if(mergable(otmp, obj)) {

The merge is done iff mergeable() returns true. Eg. if mergeable() 
returns true the two objects _are_ merged.

The function mergeable() is in invent.c (lines 2320-2395):

2320: STATIC_OVL boolean
2321: mergable(otmp, obj)	/* returns TRUE if obj  & otmp can be merged */
2322:   register struct obj *otmp, *obj;
2323: {

Satrt of the function the parameters are pointers to two objects. Like 
the comment says this function returns TRUE if these two objects can be 
merged otherwise FALSE:

2324:   if (obj->otyp != otmp->otyp) return FALSE;

If the objects aren't same type then they cannot be merged.

2325:#ifdef GOLDOBJ
2326:   /* coins of the same kind will always merge */
2327:   if (obj->oclass == COIN_CLASS) return TRUE;
2328:#endif

We're not intereested on gold, but GOLDOBJ isn't even defined in vanilla 
  3.4.3 Nethack so these lines are not compiled in.

2329:   if (obj->unpaid != otmp->unpaid ||

If-statement in C works with short-circuit. This condition is series of 
expressions separated with or's. So when first of these expressions is 
TRUE then the rest are ignored and the statement on line 2344 is 
executed ie. the items are not mergable.

The first test is unpaidness ie. both are either paid or unpaid.

2330:       obj->spe != otmp->spe || obj->dknown != otmp->dknown ||

Items enchantment or whatever spe is used for the item gas to match. So 
has the knowledge of description (color or text).

2331:       (obj->bknown != otmp->bknown && !Role_if(PM_PRIEST)) ||

BUC-status unless the hero is a priest(ess).

2332:       obj->cursed != otmp->cursed || obj->blessed != otmp->blessed ||

Both must be cursed, blessed or neither.

2333:       obj->no_charge != otmp->no_charge ||

Both must be something that shopkeeper either wants or doesn't want to buy.

2334:       obj->obroken != otmp->obroken ||

Both must be either broken or not. This field is not used for stackable 
items in 3.4.3 but it may be in the future so the check is already there.

2335:       obj->otrapped != otmp->otrapped ||

Both must be trapped or not. This same field is used for poisoned.

2336:       obj->lamplit != otmp->lamplit ||

Lightsources has to have the same lit-status.

2337:#ifdef INVISIBLE_OBJECTS
2338:       obj->oinvis != otmp->oinvis ||
2339:#endif

Invisible objects. Not implemented but this would mean that invisible 
objects would stack with visible. Makes sense.

2340:       obj->greased != otmp->greased ||

Both graesed or not.

2341:       obj->oeroded != otmp->oeroded ||

Both have same level of first erosion.

2342:       obj->oeroded2 != otmp->oeroded2 ||

Both have same level of second erosion.

2343:       obj->bypass != otmp->bypass)

Both have same bypass-status. Relevant in bhito().

